# Cell: ArcMutex · String · concat · spawn-sync · post-Track-D+E-fix `hello world-forced!` (C backend + LLVM)
#
# Graduated from known_gaps/ by Round XXXII Track D+E. Pre-fix BOTH LANES
# were broken DIFFERENTLY on this shape:
#   Rust (both backends): exit 101 ICE (move-follow-through violation)
#   Self-host: compiled, then exit 134 `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`
#
# ⚠ ONLY THE RUST LANE IS FIXED THIS ROUND. The SH sub-track (retire the
# `< PRIM_COUNT` gate at `tests/fixtures/self_host_lowerer/lower_stmt.gg:62-66`)
# was attempted (commit 9f822ea7) and REVERTED (commit b0f716c4) after the
# brief's premise that SH is "reference-grade via op_consume" was
# empirically falsified: retiring the gate exposed a SH `op_consume`
# non-scalar-inner bug (returns OpCopy on a resource String source where
# OpMove is required, triggering the SH's own validate_resource_moves
# FATAL). The SH lane on this fixture therefore STILL silently lowers
# `shared String` as plain `String` (via the `< PRIM_COUNT` fall-through)
# and STILL double-frees at exit 134 under `spawn`. See the SH follow-up
# TODO filed in TODO.md ("SH's `lower_shared_var_decl` doesn't handle
# non-scalar inners") for the fix scope.
#
# The Rust-lane class-fix is:
#   Rust: `lower_shared_var_decl`'s three resource arms omitted the
#       Move-follow-through pattern. Fix at
#       `src/ir/lowering/context.rs::materialize_addressable` +
#       `assign_with_move_follow_through`; class-retiring lint at
#       `tests/lints.rs::shared_var_decl_arms_route_through_materialize_addressable`.
#
# Because `tests/integration.rs::run_gg` uses the C backend, the integration
# test asserts C-backend output only; the SH-lane double-free remains until
# the SH `op_consume` follow-up lands.
#
# The outer `print(s)` is wrapped in `with s:` (the doc-compliant way per
# `docs/language-reference.md:1366`, even though the bare-read reject is
# not yet enforced -- filed as an owner-reconciliation follow-up in
# TODO.md). The inner `s = s + "!"` inside `bump(String &s)` is a bare
# write, but `s` there is a `String &` param NOT a shared local, so a
# `with s:` wrap there would be a type error.

async void bump(String &s):
    s = s + "!"

async void main():
    shared String s = "hello " + "world" + "-forced"
    Task[void] t = spawn bump(&s)
    t.await()
    with s:
        print(s)
